A||||5||saleenfiero||Do I need a noise filter?||||10||
Z||000000||saleenfiero||03-22-2001||08:55 PM||||Just added a new crossover to my crossover-less amps setup.  Now I have MAJOR alternator noise coming through only seems to happen when the x-overs plugged in.  Am I forgetting something?  Could something not be grounded or something.  Funny story add-on, was a Future Shop today was talking to a guy about this prob.  Said he had a friend with a chrome gold '84 GT but was gonna sell it for an '88GT because they had a Porsche(?!?) suspension. Nice eh?<P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||10
Z||000001||Oreif||03-22-2001||09:03 PM||||Make sure all your grounds go to the same point. The ground for the crossover should attach to the ground of the amp. Also check and make sure that the braided ground straps on the engine are attached too. Usually picking up noise is the sign of a ground loop. This is easy to do in any GM car since they use the "floating ground" method.||||reg||1
Z||000002||saleenfiero||03-22-2001||09:06 PM||||How many and where are these ground loops?<P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula||03-23-2001||08:26 AM||||I put a crossover n my old amp and could never get the noise to leave.  I had good gorunds, noise filters, everything!!!... I eventually just bought a Jensen 300w, 400w peak, and it pushes my 10" sony fine. with no noise lol)<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/yellowtored3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||03-23-2001||10:20 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by saleenfiero:<BR><B>How many and where are these ground loops?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>A "ground loop" is an electronics term. It's not something you can see. It is caused by attaching ground points at different locations and the chassis being a higher resistance. The ground wire between the 2 points now becomes an antenna to electrical interference. To stop a ground loop all items, amp, radio, and crossovers should be grounded at the same point. Do not use the chassis as ground for speaker wires or the RCA patch cables. Also, the placement of the crossovers could cause a problem if placed to close to the ECM or the battery wire going to the amp.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||03-23-2001||07:54 PM||||hey heres one Ive had since I put my car on the road 3 years ago. Ive got a Olympus tape deck from radio shack (ya, im not into the sound thing) when its on if I turn something on like the a/c or heater or even touch the brake pedal, I get one (1) 'click'or 'pop' in the speakers, very faint but all the time. I used the stock radio hot and ground and ran both speaker wires to radio........<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 03-23-2001).]||||reg||1
